 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets for delivery.** Committed revisions are pushed to browsers over server-managed WebSocket connections. Five-second HTTPS polling was rejected for its latency and repeated read load. A hosted realtime service (e.g. Pusher, Ably) was rejected to avoid vendor coupling and recurring per-message fees. Self-hosted WebSockets require owning connection capacity and reconnect logic but meet the freshness goal without third-party dependency. See `docs/decisions/websocket-delivery.md`.
